This chart compares how families use their income in 1968 and 2018.

Overall, spending preferences have changed between 1968 and 2018. In 1968, food was one of the most spent categories. However, food plunged nowadays and housing, leisure have skyrocketed. Household and personal goods remained the same even after 70 years.

Specifically, the percentages have risen to 19% and 22% each with housing and leisure. Transport spending rose from around 8% in 1968 to 14% in 2018. Household goods accounted for just under 10% in both 1968 and 2018, meanwhile also being the same percentage as personal goods in 1968.

In the year 1968, people almost spent one third (35%) of all of their weekly salary in food. However, only approximately 17% of a normal weekly salary was used in 2018. Furthermore, the rest of the categories in the chart mostly obtain the same percentages in 1968, i.e. about 6% to 10%